(Plan 310 task 2 - declaration position and field access. A thing name is a
 type noun everywhere the builtin ones are, so `a point called origin.` and
 `Create a point called 'the far corner'.` both declare storage sized from
 the definition, with every field taking its declared default (plan 310 §10).

 A field is an ordinary expression and an ordinary lvalue: read, `Set ... to`,
 bare assignment, increment, decrement, format-string interpolation, and a
 comparison in a condition all appear below, because §3 says a field works
 everywhere either is allowed.

 The `'bounding box'` lines were carved out of tests/330_thing_definition.vox
 when definition parsing landed on its own; they are the only place a thing
 name with a space is exercised in declaration position, and 'the far corner'
 is the only place a variable with a space is read and written through a
 possessive.

 The water tank covers the rest of the v1 field set (§6): a float and a
 boolean field, both with defaults, plus a number field with none - which
 takes its type's zero. The cursor covers a function-LOCAL thing, whose
 storage is stack rather than .bss.

 Acceptance: the thirteen lines in the .expected, in that order.)

A thing called point has
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called 'bounding box' has
  a number called width is 1,
  a number called height is 1.

A thing called 'water tank' has
  a float called 'depth in metres' is 1.5,
  a boolean called 'the pump is running' is true,
  a number called 'litres drained'.

a 'bounding box' called viewport.
Print viewport's width.

a point called origin.
Set origin's x to 3.
Set origin's y to 4.
Print origin's x.
origin's y is origin's y add 1.
increment origin's x.
Print origin's y.
Print "origin sits at {origin's x}, {origin's y}".
If origin's x is greater than 3 then,
    Print "the origin moved right".

Create a point called 'the far corner'.
Print 'the far corner''s x.
Set 'the far corner''s y to 2.
Print 'the far corner''s y.

a 'water tank' called cistern.
Print cistern's 'depth in metres'.
If cistern's 'the pump is running' then,
    Print "the pump is running".
Print cistern's 'litres drained'.
Set cistern's 'depth in metres' to 2.5.
decrement cistern's 'litres drained'.
Print cistern's 'depth in metres'.
Print cistern's 'litres drained'.

To 'plot a point'.
  a point called cursor.
  Set cursor's x to 9.
  increment cursor's y.
  Print "the cursor sits at {cursor's x}, {cursor's y}".

'plot a point'.
